ransmitter gamma aminobutyric acid (GABA) by acting on its receptors. As this site is the most prolific inhibitory receptor set within the brain, its modulation results in the sedating (or calming effects) of diclazepam on the nervous system.","source":"PsychonautWiki","page":"Diclazepam","url":"https://psychonautwiki.org/wiki/Diclazepam#Pharmacology","body":[{"what":"halfLife","says":"~42 hours"},{"what":"brokenDown","says":"Hepatic"},{"what":"leaves","says":"Renal"}],"figures":"Diclazepam"},"uses":{"text":"It is not currently approved for use as a medication, but rather sold as an unscheduled substance.","source":"Wikipedia","page":"Diclazepam","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diclazepam","routes":"Oral, sublingual","figures":"Diclazepam"}}